WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.820
Leaks often appear in failure paths.

2
00:00:02.920 --> 00:00:08.280
A function allocates one resource, fails while acquiring another, and skips cleanup.

3
00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:11.340
A constructor partially initializes an object.

4
00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:13.600
A retry path keeps old state.

5
00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:17.060
An early return misses a manual release call.

6
00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:22.200
These paths are easy to miss when tests cover only successful execution.

7
00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:28.000
RAII is the preferred answer because destructors run when owning objects leave scope.

8
00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:39.380
If a local owner represents each acquired resource, cleanup happens whether the function reaches the normal end, returns early, or exits through an exception according to project policy.

9
00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:43.740
Partial initialization needs careful ownership handling.

10
00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:48.320
A resource should not escape to other code until the owner can maintain its invariant.

11
00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:55.540
If construction cannot complete, cleanup should remain inside the object or factory that knows which pieces were acquired.

12
00:00:55.820 --> 00:01:00.380
Ownership transfer should happen only after setup has succeeded.

13
00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:06.320
A function should avoid handing responsibility to another component while still depending on local cleanup assumptions.

14
00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:12.020
Transfer points should be visible and should leave exactly one owner responsible for each resource.

15
00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:15.500
Error-path tests should verify cleanup behavior.

16
00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:25.760
Tests should exercise failed initialization, rejected inputs, canceled operations, retries, shutdown during setup, and repeated create-destroy cycles.

17
00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:30.980
Leak prevention becomes much more reliable when failure paths are first-class test cases.

18
00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:44.060
A useful implementation habit is to acquire resources into local RAII owners first, validate the rest of the setup, and then commit the completed owner to longer-lived state only after the invariant is established.
